<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Digambara</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Digambara, meaning 'sky-clad', is one of the two main traditions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a>, alongside the Svetambara. Its monks renounce all clothing, and it holds that women must be reborn as men to attain liberation and that the original scriptures were lost. Strongest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>, it flourished under the patronage of dynasties such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-ganga-dynasty">Western Gangas</a> and the Rashtrakutas, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shravanabelagola">Shravanabelagola</a> as a major centre.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/digambara.jpg" alt="Digambara" width="320" height="485" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dayodaya · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahavir.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Digambara, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> for sky-clad, names one of the two major schools of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a>, the other being Svetambara, the white-clad. Digambaras hold that they are the original followers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavira">Mahavira</a>, who taught that nakedness symbolised complete detachment and spared a monk the distraction of acquiring and washing garments. In tradition the schools divided in the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandragupta-maurya">Chandragupta Maurya</a> (c. 324-297 BCE). A twelve-year famine struck <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a>, and a group of monks led by Bhadrabahu, said to be the last who knew the complete original canon, migrated south to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> and kept to strict asceticism, including nudity. Monks who stayed in the north under Sthulabhadra took to wearing white robes. Digambaras say the northern group kept beliefs and practices they consider heretical.</p><p>The oldest archaeological evidence for Digambaras is the Mangalam inscriptions, which record stone beds made for Digambara monks by workers of the Pandyan king Netunceliyan I, around 270 BCE. Statues of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirthankara">tirthankaras</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a> dated to the second century CE or later are naked, and Svetambara iconography appears there only from the late fifth century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Digambara monks own nothing. They are wandering mendicants who carry a community-owned picchi, a broom of fallen peacock feathers used to brush insects from their path or seat so as not to kill them. The tradition shares the Jain principles of aparigraha, non-attachment, ahimsa, non-violence, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anekantavada">anekantavada</a>, the many-sidedness of reality. Digambaras maintain that women cannot attain nirvana, while Svetambaras hold that women, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuchs</a>, can. The two schools have differed in dress, temples and iconography, attitudes to female monastics, legends and the texts they treat as important.</p><p>Digambara monastic lineages trace their descent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavira">Mahavira</a>, among them the Mula Sangha and the Kashtha Sangha. For most of the second millennium CE ascetic life centred on lineages of bhattarakas. In the seventeenth century a lay movement at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a> led to the division into Terapanthi and Bisapanthi sub-sects, and the Terapanthis, led by Pandit Todarmal and Banarasidas, rejected the bhattarakas' authority.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>From about the middle of the first millennium CE the Western Ganga, Rashtrakuta and Chalukya dynasties of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> and south <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> patronised Digambara teachers. Under the Rashtrakuta dynasty the scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jinasena">Jinasena</a> advised King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amoghavarsha-i">Amoghavarsha I</a> (r. 814-878 CE), and he and his disciple <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunabhadra">Gunabhadra</a> wrote the Mahapurana. Jain caves at Ellora, such as the Indra Sabha, contain Digambara images. Kundakunda, author of the Samayasara and Pravacanasara, is regarded as the tradition's most significant scholar-monk. Digambara communities live today across India, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Digambara” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their patronage of Jain scholarship made the Deccan a major centre of <b>Digambara</b> learning.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashtrakuta-dynasty">Rashtrakuta dynasty · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Basham notes several cases in which other writers confuse them with other groups: a commentator of the Achara-sara takes them for Buddhists, and in the Neelakesi their leader asks that his followers not be mistaken for <b>Digambaras</b>.</blockquote><a href="/religion/ajivika">Ājīvika · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>In the early centuries of the Common Era a long-developing division hardened into two traditions: the <b>Digambaras</b>, “sky-clad”, whose monks renounce even clothing, and the Svetambaras, “white-clad”, whose monks and nuns wear simple white robes.</blockquote><a href="/religion/jainism">Jainism · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
